{{also|uranian}}
==English==

===Etymology===
The senses relating to sexuality are adapted from {{cog|de|Urning}}, {{m|de|Urnigtum}}, referring to {{w|Aphrodite Urania}}, coined by the German writer {{w|Karl Heinrich Ulrichs}} (1825-1895). This coinage predates the word {{m|en|homosexual}} (as {{ncog|de|Homosexualist}}, 1869).

===Pronunciation===
* {{rhymes|eɪniən|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|science fiction}} An [[inhabitant]] of the planet [[Uranus]].
# {{lb|en|very|_|dated}} A male [[homosexual]]. {{defdate|German from 1864}}
#: {{syn|en|gay|homosexual}}
#* {{quote-text|year=2003|author=Chuck Stewart|title=Gay and Lesbian Issues: A Reference Handbook|page=138|passage=Carpenter was disappointed by these meetings with Whitman but began to integrate a positive homosexual view into his socialist writings. Carpenter saw the Uranian (homosexual) spirit to be more enlightened than that of the common man and believed that '''Uranians''' were the new prophets of the coming social revolution.}}
#* {{quote-text|year=2008|author=Thomas Bohache|title=Christology From the Margins|page=194|passage=One who engages in the love that dare not speak its name. '''Uranian'''. Homosexual. Invert. Gay. Lesbian. Queer.}}

